WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.549
Round-robin routing sends requests to backends in turn.

2
00:00:03.549 --> 00:00:07.467
It is simple and can work when servers and requests are similar.

3
00:00:07.467 --> 00:00:10.646
Local LLM environments often are not that uniform.

4
00:00:10.646 --> 00:00:18.261
One server may have a stronger GPU, another may have a smaller model, and a third may already be busy with long-context work.

5
00:00:18.261 --> 00:00:22.475
Round-robin is easy to start with, but it is not capacity awareness.

6
00:00:22.475 --> 00:00:29.055
Least-connections routing sends new work toward the backend with fewer active connections or requests.

7
00:00:29.055 --> 00:00:33.047
That can be better than round-robin, but it still needs caution.

8
00:00:33.047 --> 00:00:37.631
One long generation can consume more resources than several short calls.

9
00:00:37.631 --> 00:00:42.215
A streaming client may hold a connection open while tokens arrive slowly.

10
00:00:42.215 --> 00:00:46.207
Connection count is a signal, not a complete performance model.

11
00:00:46.207 --> 00:00:51.382
Weighted routing lets stronger or lighter-loaded backends receive more traffic.

12
00:00:51.382 --> 00:01:01.807
Health-check-based routing prevents traffic from going to servers that are down, still loading a model, repeatedly failing, or not ready for the requested workload.

13
00:01:01.807 --> 00:01:10.309
Good health checks are more than checking whether a port is open; they should reflect whether the backend can actually serve the request.

14
00:01:10.309 --> 00:01:19.329
More mature setups add model-aware routing, endpoint-aware routing, queues, failover, request timeouts, retry policies, and backpressure.

15
00:01:19.329 --> 00:01:24.947
Model-aware routing confirms that the requested model or capability exists on a backend.

16
00:01:24.947 --> 00:01:30.049
Backpressure tells clients the service is busy instead of allowing slow failure.

17
00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:35.815
The routing strategy should reflect real capacity, model availability, and workload type.
